<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mihail Gerdzhikov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mihail Gerdzhikov was a Bulgarian revolutionary of anarchist sympathies and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization">Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</a>. During the Ilinden-Preobrazhenie uprising of August 1903 he led the insurgents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> mountains, where they set up a short-lived self-governing administration later called the Strandzha Commune. Ottoman forces crushed it within about three weeks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mihail-gerdzhikov.jpg" alt="Mihail Gerdzhikov" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mihail-Gerdzhikov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mihail Gerdzhikov was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and studied at the French College there, where he was nicknamed Michel. In 1893, still a pupil, he began his revolutionary work as leader of a Macedonian Secret Revolutionary Committee. As a student in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> he took part in the so-called Geneva Group. In 1899 he moved to Ottoman Macedonia, taught at the Bulgarian Men's High School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and joined the Internal Macedonian Adrianople Revolutionary Organization, where he grew close to Gotse Delchev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was a delegate to the Seventh Congress of the Supreme Macedonian-Adrianople Committee in 1900 and to its Eighth Congress in April 1901. After the defeat of the Strandzha Commune in the Preobrazhenie Uprising he saw to the settling of the rebels who withdrew from Ottoman Thrace into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and wrote in the Bulgarian and foreign press calling for international intervention to settle the Eastern question. With Varban Kilifarski he also published newspapers of their own.</p><p>When the Balkan War broke out in 1912 he led the Lozengrad guerrilla unit of the Macedonian-Adrianopolitan Volunteer Corps. He was then mobilised into the Bulgarian Army and served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> with the Forty-third Infantry Regiment. After the war he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization">IMRO</a> (United) and in 1930 attended its conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he became a member of its Central Committee, but he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1931 instead of going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his later years Gerdzhikov worked as a journalist and translator and wrote for a number of periodicals. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he kept apart from the resistance movement, though some of his associates took part in it and he kept up ties with them. After the coup of 9 September he signed an appeal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonians-ethnic-group">Macedonians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. He died in 1947, disappointed with the new communist authorities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mihail Gerdzhikov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leaders influenced by anarchist ideas, notably <b>Mihail Gerdzhikov</b>, organised local self-government, supplies and defence, and promised fair treatment to Greek and Turkish villagers.</blockquote><a href="/state/strandzha-commune">Strandzha Commune</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.14,24.74&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Plovdiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5151 664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mihail Gerdzhikov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5601 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5601 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.14,24.74&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
